  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal Express obligated $346M in FY2026 to date. Federal Express won 912 awards this fiscal year to date with an average award size of $379K. Obligations fell 40.5% year over year. Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer in FY2026 to date, obligating $193M, followed by Department of Defense at $135M. Couriers and Express Delivery Services is the leading market, accounting for $345M in obligations. Historical totals show variability: FY21 was $305M, FY22 was $309M, FY23 was $275M, FY24 was $677M, and FY25 was $582M. Some NAICS categories show minimal or zero obligations, including Testing Laboratories and Services at $0. Current-year totals are partial because FY2026 is fiscal year to date. Award-value tables use total contract values while other figures are contract obligations. This snapshot highlights where Federal Express is selling to government customers and the scale of obligations through the current fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why did Federal Express obligations change year over year?",
        "body": "Federal Express obligations fell 40.5% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show a peak in FY24 at $677M and a decline to $346M in FY26 to date. The distribution of FY2026 obligations concentrates with the Department of Veterans Affairs and Department of Defense, which are the two largest buying agencies this fiscal year to date. The data do not assign causes beyond those year-to-year totals."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where are Federal Express obligations concentrated in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Department of Veterans Affairs and Department of Defense are the top two buyers in FY2026 to date, with obligations of $193M and $135M respectively. Couriers and Express Delivery Services is the dominant NAICS market with $345M in obligations. Other listed markets include Nonscheduled Chartered Passenger Air Transportation at $1.5M and Testing Laboratories and Services at $0."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
